Lemmings (RISC OS/Acorn Archimedes, 1992, Krisalis) - reverse engineering and
static recompilation, following the same approach as ../galactic dan.

Disc images
-----------
"Lemmings (1991)(Krisalis).adf"      819200 bytes, ADFS-E format (800K "new
                                      map", old-style small directories).
                                      Despite the .adf extension this is an
                                      Acorn ADFS floppy image, not Amiga.
"Lemmings (1991)(Krisalis)[a].adf"   814080 bytes, alternate dump, not yet
                                      investigated.

Unlike Galactic Dan's disc, this one is NOT copy protected: no encrypted
loader, no protection module/track. !Run just RMLoads two modules and runs
a boot file in the clear.

tools/                  ADFSlib.py (github.com/dcf21/adfslib, vendored) reads
                         the ADFS-E layout correctly; tools/adfsd_old.py
                         (galactic dan's old-map extractor) does NOT work on
                         this disc - wrong map format, kept only for
                         reference. extract.py rebuilds extracted/ from the
                         .adf. arm2.py is galactic dan's ARM2 emulator (used
                         both for the notes above and, now, for real work -
                         see unpack_lemboot.py below).

extracted/              !LEMMINGS/ application tree, as stored on disc:
                           !Run, !RunCold      Obey files (see below)
                           !Sprites, Boot      icon sprites / boot options
                           LemBoot             4.5K loader, RISC OS absolute
                                               load &8000. First &2000 bytes
                                               of itself get copied to
                                               &40000 (loop at &8010-&802c)
                                               and it continues running from
                                               there - which is what makes it
                                               safe to then OS_File-load
                                               LemProg *at* &8000, clobbering
                                               the vacated low copy of
                                               itself. Also loads/unpacks
                                               Krisalis, PSYGNO and DMALOGO
                                               the same way (not wired up
                                               yet, see below).
                           MemMod              relocatable module, RMLoaded
                                               by !Run before anything else
                                               ("RMensure TequeMemory 0
                                               RmLoad ... MemMod").
                           musmod2             sound driver module (RMLoaded
                                               as MusicModule2).
                           LRestMouse          tiny (56 byte) helper, run
                                               after the game exits.
                           Lemmings/           DmaLogo (intro?), Fred (4
                                               bytes - a flag?), Krisalis
                                               (splash?), LemProg (68772
                                               bytes on disc, compressed -
                                               see extracted_decompressed/),
                                               OddTable, Psygno (sound
                                               engine?), SampdatP, LemGrafP,
                                               plus:
                             LemGrafix/        ArcGrn1-5P, ArcObj1-5P,
                                               Arcpan1p, SPECIAL0-3 - sprite/
                                               graphics data. The "P" suffix
                                               plus LemBoot's unpacker
                                               strongly suggests these are
                                               compressed too (not confirmed
                                               yet - LemBoot only references
                                               Krisalis/PSYGNO/DMALOGO/
                                               LemProg by name, so either
                                               these are unpacked by a
                                               different piece of code, by
                                               LemProg itself at runtime, or
                                               under a name we haven't
                                               matched to a file yet).
                             LemLevels/        LEMMUS0/1, LEVEL000-024,
                                               LEVELDAT, MUSINFO - level
                                               data, one file per level.

extracted_decompressed/ Decompressed game files, built by tools/unpack_*.py.
                           LemProg.bin         147264 bytes, load &8000 -
                                               the REAL main engine, in
                                               plain ARM2 code (confirmed by
                                               disassembling start/middle/
                                               end - all coherent, no
                                               garbage). 2.14x the on-disc
                                               size. This is what
                                               ghidra/run_ghidra_lemprog.sh
                                               analyses, and it's a normal
                                               ARM2 binary - despite the
                                               name "LemProg" sounding like
                                               it could hide an embedded
                                               68000 emulator (this being a
                                               port from the Amiga/ST 68k
                                               original), it doesn't: this
                                               is native ARM code, written
                                               specifically for this port.
                           Krisalis.bin        32000 bytes, load &8000 - a
                           PSYGNO.bin          320x200 4bpp (16-colour, two
                           DMALOGO.bin         pixels/byte, high nibble
                                               first) splash bitmap - this
                                               is an Amiga-derived port, so
                                               16 colours, not 256; an
                                               initial 8bpp/320x100 guess
                                               rendered as a duplicated,
                                               squashed image (32000 bytes
                                               also happens to divide as
                                               320x100 8bpp) and was wrong.
                                               Rendered and visually
                                               confirmed at the correct
                                               dimensions (see docs/
                                               *_preview.png): DMALOGO is the
                                               DMA Design logo, PSYGNO is the
                                               Psygnosis logo, Krisalis.bin
                                               is the Krisalis logo. No
                                               palette recovered yet - previews
                                               are rendered as grayscale
                                               (nibble value x17).

ghidra/                  Carried over from galactic dan and confirmed
                          working on real code:
                            spider.py         recursive-descent + speculative
                                               code finder. One fix on top of
                                               galactic dan's version - see
                                               "Fixing spider's false negative"
                                               below.
                            export_ghidra.py  spider -> json (unmodified)
                            gen_asm.py        json -> relocatable GNU as
                                               (unmodified, not yet run here)
                            run_ghidra_lemboot.sh / run_ghidra_lemprog.sh
                                              import LemBoot / LemProg.bin
                                              into Ghidra at &8000, apply the
                                              spider's classification,
                                              decompile.
                                              `GHIDRA=/path ./run_ghidra_lemprog.sh`
                                              (defaults to the same
                                              ghidra_12.1.3_PUBLIC install
                                              galactic dan uses). Output
                                              goes to out/lemboot/ and
                                              out/lemprog/ (galdan.c/
                                              functions.tsv - filenames are
                                              hardcoded by
                                              RiscOSPostAnalysis.java).
                          LemBoot: 28 functions decompiled, spider/ghidra
                          agree on 2020/2072 code bytes.
                          LemProg.bin: 199 functions decompiled (up from an
                          initial 149 before the spider fix below), overall
                          code coverage 12.0% -> 19.6% of the image, ghidra/
                          spider agree on 25492/28804 code bytes.

asm/, recomp/            Empty so far - this is where galactic dan's
                          reassembler and SDL3 static recompiler live; not
                          started here.

How LemProg gets decompressed
------------------------------
tools/unpack_lemboot.py doesn't reimplement LemBoot's LZ-style codec by
hand - it *emulates LemBoot itself* (via arm2.py) far enough to load and
unpack the real file, which sidesteps every chance of a hand-transcription
bug in a bit-level compression algorithm. Concretely, for LemProg:
  - LemBoot's relocated copy (see above) contains a small function at file
    offset &8aac that: sets r8 = pointer to the string "LemProg", r9 (APCS
    "sb") = &8000 (target load address), then calls a shared loader.
  - The loader issues `OS_File 255` ("load object at the given address")
    for "<Lem$Dir>.Lemmings.LemProg" - our emulated SWI handler resolves
    that RISC OS path against extracted/ (case-insensitively, stripping the
    ",load-exec" suffix extract.py adds) and copies the real file's bytes
    into emulated memory at &8000.
  - It then calls the unpacker at file offset &8468 (the same LZ-style
    routine visible from &84a4-&8620 in a disassembly - self-modifying,
    reads the compressed stream backwards from high to low addresses).
  - We run the emulator until it returns, tracking every memory write (via
    arm2.py's write_hook) to discover the true decompressed extent, then
    save that span as the output file - no assumptions about the codec's
    internals required, only about *which registers/strings set up the
    call*, which we got from reading the disassembly once.
Krisalis/PSYGNO/DMALOGO use a second, near-identical call site (file offset
&8af0, shared by all three) that reads r9 from a literal-pool slot at &8a20
instead of using a fixed constant - real LemBoot presumably fills that slot
in dynamically before each call (not traced), but all three decompress
cleanly when we pre-seed it to &8000 ourselves, same as LemProg's target.
The result for each is a 320x200 4bpp (16-colour) bitmap that renders as
the expected logo (see docs/*_preview.png) - about as strong a correctness
check on the whole codec-by-emulation approach as we could ask for.

LemGrafix/LemLevels are NOT loaded by LemBoot at all - grepping strings out
of the decompressed LemProg.bin turns up "LemGrafix.ArcGrn1P",
"LemLevels.LEVEL000" etc. around file offset &2be00-&2bf40 (right near the
end of LemProg's own data). Found the actual loader by searching the whole
binary for 4-byte words equal to each string's address (i.e. finding literal
pool references rather than relying on spider/Ghidra to have already
classified the caller as reached code - the calling function, around
&27ad0-&27ca8, is genuinely valid code that spider's speculative pass
missed, most likely because it's reached only via a jump table or function
pointer we haven't found the source of yet). Each call site: loads r8 =
string pointer via the same "bl to a fixed thunk, literal word follows"
trick LemBoot uses (&8074 = load-into-r8 thunk, &8080 = load-into-sb
thunk), patches 1-3 digit bytes into the string (level number / variant
selection - LEVEL000's digits sit at string offsets &f-&11), sets sb = a
fixed target address (LEVEL data -> &4773c/&4973c, ArcObj1P -> &6a83c,
the ArcGrn/arcpan group -> &77b2c - all comfortably above LemProg's own
&8000-&2bf40 code+data, i.e. these get their own permanent buffers rather
than being chain-loaded over running code), then tail-jumps into a loader
at &280e0 that is *the same OS_File-255-load template* LemBoot uses
(&2811c builds "<Lem$Dir>.Lemmings.<name>", svc &20008 loads it) - but,
unlike LemBoot's version, &280e0 does NOT call an unpacker afterward.
That's a real finding, not just an oversight: LEVEL000's raw on-disc bytes
don't look like the LZ-compressed streams we saw for LemProg/Krisalis/etc.
(no long zero run, fairly uniform byte distribution) and 1332 bytes is
in the right ballpark for the well-documented classic Lemmings binary
level format (terrain pieces + object placements) stored directly. So the
"P" suffix on the LemGrafix filenames most likely does NOT mean
"LZ-packed" the way DMALOGO/PSYGNO/Krisalis were - more likely a planar/
packed-pixel bitmap encoding specific to the graphics code, unrelated to
LemBoot's unpacker. Reading LemProg's sprite-drawing code (not yet
attempted) would confirm this either way.

Fixing spider's false negative on the &27a60 loader dispatch
--------------------------------------------------------------
The &27ad0-&27ca8 caller wasn't in spider's output at all initially, and
adding it as an explicit entry point (export_ghidra.py's `[extra-entry]`
args) didn't help either - the very first instruction after the entry,
&27a64, tripped spider's `valid_arm2()` check ('NV condition' = invalid)
and the whole trace was thrown away.

That word (bytes 00 00 a0 f1, i.e. "mov r0,r0" with its condition field
forced to NV = "never execute") turns out to be a real, pervasive idiom in
this codebase: "stmdb sp!, {pc}" (push just PC - a hand-rolled fake return
address, standing in for a BL whose target is out of B/BL's +-32MB range)
is always immediately followed by exactly this NV filler word, then the
real "b target". Nothing on ARM2 ever executes an NV-conditioned
instruction, so it's inert - but spider's gap analysis (correctly, for its
original job of scoring "does this look like code") flagged it as
implausible, and its recursive-descent tracer (incorrectly, for actually
disassembling this codebase) aborted the whole function on it.

We'd already seen this exact 3-word shape work fine dozens of times
elsewhere (every register-load vector thunk in LemBoot uses it) - it just
happened that in those cases spider always arrived at the idiom via an
already-established trace rather than as a fresh function entry, and
apparently never via *speculative* entry either, so the bug never showed
up as a rejected trace before.

Fixed in spider.py's LDM/STM handling: recognise "stmdb sp!, {pc}"
(register list == {pc} exactly) followed by an NV word followed by a real
B/BL, and skip the filler word instead of decoding it. Verified this isn't
over-matching ordinary data that happens to start the same way (checked
with and without also requiring the trailing B/BL - identical results
either way, so the trailing check is a safety margin, not load-bearing).

Effect on LemProg.bin alone: speculative code coverage 12.0% -> 19.6% of
the whole image, functions found 149 -> 221 (199 -> Ghidra-decompiled,
Ghidra's own jump-table warnings roughly doubled too - expected, since a
lot of the newly-found code is exactly this kind of hand-written indirect
dispatch that Ghidra can't auto-resolve, not a sign the new code is wrong).
LemBoot picked up a smaller, consistent improvement (23 -> 28 functions).
This is very likely THE reason large parts of LemProg were unreachable in
the first place, not a one-off - the same macro/idiom is presumably used
everywhere in this codebase that a far branch or call is needed.

Status / next steps
--------------------
1. Extraction: DONE (tools/extract.py, via ADFSlib).
2. LemBoot: DONE - spider+Ghidra pipeline runs clean, and we've read enough
   of the decompile to fully understand its relocate-then-chainload
   behaviour and how it invokes the unpacker for LemProg, Krisalis, PSYGNO
   and DMALOGO (see above; all four decompress and, for the three images,
   visually check out).
3. LemProg: DONE for this stage - decompressed (extracted_decompressed/
   LemProg.bin, 147264 bytes) and imported into Ghidra (205 functions,
   see above). This is the real engine, load &8000, entry point at file
   offset &192ac (i.e. runtime address &192ac). It is NOT 68000 code (it's
   a native ARM2 rewrite, not an emulated Amiga/ST port) - confirmed by
   disassembling the start, a middle section, and the very end of the
   decompressed buffer, all clean ARM2 with no garbage or 68k-style
   patterns.
4. MemMod and musmod2 are RISC OS *modules*, not absolute-loaded code - they
   have a different header format (module header with offsets to title,
   help string, SWI table, entry points) and get relocated at RMLoad time.
   spider.py/export_ghidra.py assume absolute load; will need adapting for
   the module header and for being entered via multiple SWI/vector entry
   points rather than one.
5. LemGrafix/*P and LemLevels/* loading: DONE for this stage - found the
   loader (LemProg file offset &280e0, called via &27ad0-&27ca8) by
   searching the binary for literal-pool references to the string
   addresses rather than relying on spider/Ghidra's reachability analysis,
   since that code wasn't in the (at the time) 205 already-decompiled
   functions. Chasing *why* it wasn't reachable led to the spider.py fix
   above, which pulled the whole &27a60-&2be24 region (and a lot more
   elsewhere) into the decompile properly - see ghidra/out/lemprog/galdan.c
   around those addresses for the real, now-named functions instead of the
   manual disassembly notes above. It's the same OS_File-255 template as
   LemBoot's loader, but with no unpack call - these assets most likely
   aren't LZ-compressed at all (see above).
   NEXT: run tools/unpack_lemboot.py's technique against LemProg.bin itself
   (driving arm2.py from its &280e0 entry, same OS_File SWI shim) to
   actually load a level + its graphics into a file - not needed to prove
   the format (we're not decoding it right now), but needed to get the raw
   bytes out for anything downstream. Actual level-format decoding is
   explicitly deferred per current priorities (finding code > decoding
   data).
6. Reassembly (asm/lemprog, asm/lemboot): DONE - both reassemble
   byte-identical to the originals (lemmings.s currently 0 .inst fallbacks,
   lemboot.s 0).
7. Static recompilation to C (recomp/): DONE for this stage, reusing
   galactic dan's recomp/ wholesale (runtime.h is verbatim - it's pure ARM2
   semantics; gen_c.py's translator logic is unmodified, only its
   hardcoded paths were parametrized). One deliberate departure from
   galactic dan: instead of silently falling back to a full interpreter for
   any untranslated address (galactic dan's approach), call_addr() logs and
   exits immediately by default - see "Fixing spider's false negatives"
   below for why, and --trace-gaps for the diagnostic escape hatch.
   Found and fixed three more instances of the same root problem as the
   &27a60 fix above (spider/gen_c.py assuming ordinary call-return
   semantics on idioms that don't use it - see git log for the three
   commits). The recompiled binary now runs 100,000,000 instructions
   headless (0 gaps, 0 interpreted) and 4Bn+ instructions in a real
   interactive SDL run before being killed - i.e. the entire startup/init/
   main-loop path is fully statically translated.
8. Screen mode: IN PROGRESS. Confirmed 4bpp/16-colour (matches the Amiga
   original, not a 256-colour Archimedes mode) and confirmed LemProg
   programs VIDC's palette registers *directly* (physical address
   &3400000), bypassing the OS entirely - see "Screen mode and the VIDC
   palette split" below. The exact trigger for the mid-screen panel/game
   palette split (a self-modifying vector-chain install we haven't fully
   read yet) isn't understood, so the real palette values aren't visible
   yet, but forcing a test palette confirms the pixel-unpacking geometry is
   right: the screen memory clearly shows what looks like Lemmings' skill-
   icon panel (a row of 5-ish distinct icon-shaped blocks under a
   repeating top band), not noise.
9. Not started: MemMod and musmod2 (RISC OS *modules*, different header
   format, entered via multiple SWI/vector points rather than one -
   spider.py/gen_asm.py assume absolute load code and would need adapting);
   sound (musmod2's own SWI chunk is stubbed to log-and-continue, see
   runtime.c); input handling for actual gameplay; LemGrafix/LemLevels
   asset loading and format (found but not pursued - see above).

This is comparable in scope to galactic dan, possibly larger (multiple
cooperating modules instead of one blob, plus a real level-data format to
reverse), but with no copy protection to defeat first.

Screen mode and the VIDC palette split
---------------------------------------
Confirmed in ghidra/out/lemprog/lemmings.c (spec_852c @ &852c): a 16-word
table gets loaded into VIDC's palette registers via
`_DAT_03400000 = *puVar2 | iVar3 << 0x1a` (value | register<<26 is VIDC's
real register-write format; registers 0-15 are the 16 palette entries,
4 bits each of R/G/B - 4bpp, matching the Amiga original, not a 256-colour
Archimedes mode). There are *two* such tables (&8428 and &83e8, selected by
an if/else on real IOC hardware interrupt-status bits at &3200014/
&3200024) - this is exactly the mid-screen "copper style" panel/game
palette split you'd expect, confirmed rather than guessed.

Both tables read as all-zero statically - they're runtime variables,
populated during startup, not baked into the image. tools/ doesn't have a
way to recover them without running the game, so recomp/runtime.c now
intercepts physical writes to &3400000 (WR32 in runtime.h checks for it,
runtime.c's vidc_write() records the live palette into vidc_palette[16] and
logs any non-palette VIDC register write - none seen yet - to vidc.log) and
display.c reads vidc_palette every frame instead of using a fixed table.

UPDATE: spec_852c has zero callers - confirmed both in
ghidra/out/lemprog/functions.tsv (callers=0) and by instrumenting every
instruction executed (dbg_hook() in runtime.h/.c, still present but
currently a no-op): never once reached in 100M instructions. The &2a6bc
install sequence writes into absolute low workspace &100, not through
OS_Claim/Event_V like the &2a70c handler (which *is* correctly claimed and
called, confirmed via OS_Claim in the event log) - &100 is almost
certainly a lower-level, direct hardware-IRQ dispatch slot the kernel
itself reads on every interrupt, which we don't emulate (we only simulate
the OS_Claim/Event_V layer, not the kernel's own internal IRQ chain).

Rather than reverse-engineer that kernel internal, runtime.c's rt_service()
now calls spec_852c directly every frame (call_852c(), bit 6/flyback set,
bit 3 clear - the branch that does an early return before falling through
to a chain-handler call we're not sure is safely initialised). This DOES
fire correctly now (vidc.log shows all 16 registers being written every
frame, right on the per-frame cadence) - but every value is zero. The
*mechanism* is right; the *source table* (&8428) is still unpopulated at
the point we're calling it.

UPDATE: it wasn't MusicModule2 - musmod2_swi() is never even called (see
below; retracted). recomp.events showed only three OS_File loads ever
happening (LemGrafP, LemLevels.Musinfo, sampdatp) because execution was
genuinely stuck in a busy-wait, not because it was done making progress.

Diagnosed with a PC histogram (dbg_hook()/pchist[] in runtime.c/.h - a
temporary, cheap-per-instruction hook, still present but harmless): after
the third file load, the CPU spent effectively all its time spinning on
`ldr r0,[pc,#-0x120]; ldr r1,[pc,#-0x128]; orrs r0,r0,r1; ...; bne` at
&8600/&dc28 - a generic "wait while [&84e8]|[&84ec] is nonzero" primitive,
called from 9+ sites across &d000-&19000. Both addresses get set to &ff by
two separate "kick" sites (&dc0c, &198f4) and are never cleared anywhere
in the static image - the clearing must be a side effect of real hardware
we don't emulate (most likely sound DMA buffer-end, given the proximity to
sound asset loading, but not confirmed). Musmod2's own module header
(SWIchunk &41c40, SWI names Mus_On/Off/Songpos/Getpos/Volume/Samplerate/
Patch/Effect/Effectstatus/Load/Effectcontrol/Run/Sector/Bufferaddress/
Memvalid/Laidback/Samplestarts, read straight from its SWIdecode table at
file offset &5e8) turned out to be a dead end for this particular hang:
LemProg never calls a single one of its SWIs in the runs where this loop
was hit.

Fix (runtime.h's WR32, clearly marked EXPERIMENTAL): treat &84e8/&84ec as
write-only "always reads back zero" - i.e. simulate the hardware
completing whatever it's being asked to do instantly. This unblocked
forward progress: the game now reaches a real, correctly-vsync-
synchronised main loop (confirmed: the *next* hottest loop, at &2a598,
turned out to be a legitimate "wait for next tick" primitive - its counter
genuinely is incremented every frame by the already-working &2a70c Event_V
handler - our emulation just runs so much faster than real ARM2 hardware
that it spins millions of times between each unlock, which is expected,
not a bug).

UPDATE - found the actual "Start" hotspot, and it works: read the real
mouse-handling code directly (the only `svc &1c` (OS_Mouse) in the whole
image, at &2a834) rather than guessing screen positions blind. It scales
OS_Mouse's X (0-1279) by /4 -> pixel 0-319 (confirms 320 wide), and Y via
(1024-Y)/4 clamped at 0 (confirms the OS-unit vertical flip). A second
function at &dea8 gates on scaled Y in [148,187) then dispatches on X
across six ~50-70px-wide slots; the [22,78) slot falls straight into the
&27a60 loader chain we fixed earlier in this project. Clicking OS_Mouse
coordinates (200,364) - i.e. scaled pixel (50,165), inside that slot -
reliably (reproduced twice) triggers real level/asset loading:
MusMod2 On/Load/Run calls, then OS_File loads for LemLevels.LEVEL018,
LemGrafix.ArcObj1P, LemGrafix.ArcGrn1P, LemGrafix.arcpan1p. This is a real, working "start game" input, not a guess anymore:
`./lemmings --script=3000:click:200:364,3100:release,...` (interactive
mode only - see below).

Caveat found along the way: --script/--headless don't work together.
headless's irq_point() path doesn't call rt_service() at all (that's
where run_script() lives), so --script is silently ignored under
--headless - inherited from galactic dan, not something we changed. Use
interactive mode (needs a real or virtual display) for scripted input.

RESOLVED - spec_852c was a red herring. Added a genuine write-watchpoint
(watch_write() in runtime.c/.h, hooked into WR32/WR8) on &83e0-&8467
instead of more static guessing: confirmed nothing, ever, writes into that
range in any run, including well past the level-load sequence above -
it's dead/vestigial code, not the real mechanism.

The real mechanism was hiding in plain sight in recomp.events the whole
time: `OS_Word 12` (the standard, documented RISC OS "write palette entry"
SWI - R1 -> {logical colour, 3-byte physical RGB}) fires regularly from
early on, and we'd been logging it without implementing it. Implemented
properly in swi()'s case 7: write straight into vidc_palette[colour & 0xF].
Also removed the call_852c() experiment entirely (spec_852c is simply
never used by this build - not a bug in our emulation, the game just
doesn't call it).

Result: real, correctly-decoded colour on screen. A ~40-second run past
the start click shows the skill-icon panel with its actual palette - a
dark red/maroon background (colour 0 = &18 -> RGB(136,17,0)), matching
what Lemmings' control panel should look like. Only colour 0 has been
observed being set so far in the runs we've tried (screen is otherwise
still black/colour-0 where other logical colours would apply) - either
the other 15 palette-setting calls happen even later than we've run for,
or there's a second piece to this (e.g. a bulk multi-colour variant, or
the remaining colours are set once during a slower asset-decode step we
haven't waited long enough for) - not yet confirmed either way.

Found a second, real bug this way: reported (not yet reproduced by us -
our own runs still show the same panel screen above) as a "scrambled"
display, not the expected logo/menu. vduvar()/the OS_ReadModeVariable
mv[] table were still galactic dan's inherited 320x256 *8bpp* constants
(NColour=255, LineLength=320 bytes/row, ScreenSize=81920, XEig/YEig=1,
bank stride &14000) - all wrong for Lemmings' actual 320x200 *4bpp* mode.
Concretely, NColour (var 3) and ScrBRow/var 12 used to be the same
inherited value (255) - true for galactic dan by coincidence (256 colours
and 256-tall screen both give 255) but they diverge for Lemmings (15 vs
199), so anything reading NColour to size a colour-lookup table would
have been overrunning by 16x. Fixed all of these (see vduvar()/mv[]/the
bank-stride constant in runtime.c) using values we could independently
verify: XEig=YEig=2 is *confirmed*, not guessed, since it's the same /4
factor the OS_Mouse scaling code (&2a834, used to find the start button)
already proved. Not yet confirmed this fixes the reported scrambling
specifically, since we can't reproduce it yet - most likely relevant to
whatever real gameplay/scrolling rendering looks like, which our own
testing hasn't reached (we've only gotten as far as the panel above).

UPDATE - reproduced it (screenshot: scrambled.png) and found the real
cause: it's immediate (present before any click) and never changes, which
rules out a dynamically-drawn broken image - it's a *static* leftover
state. Repurposed watch_write() (runtime.c/.h) to watch SCREEN itself
instead of spec_852c's tables, and caught the literal byte pattern
`dddddddd` being written straight into screen memory by a copy routine at
&e1e0-&e228 (and &16fe8-&16ffc) - 0xDD is a classic "uninitialised memory"
fill convention. This isn't a stride bug corrupting real pixels; it's a
real blit of a source buffer that was never populated with actual decoded
graphics.

Traced the copy's source pointer (fp+&124, read at &19360) back through
two more indirections (&192d4-&192dc, &16fc0-&16fc8) to a fixed workspace
slot at &8008 - which gets *read* (via the same register-thunk idiom used
everywhere else in this codebase) in multiple places but is never
*written* anywhere we've found. Most likely explanation: &8008 is meant
to hold a free-memory/workspace pointer RISC OS itself would normally
provide (via OS_GetEnv's memory-limit return, or a WimpSlot-derived
value) that our runtime's stub for that doesn't faithfully replicate -
not a simple constant-typo bug like the last two fixes, but a real gap in
how we model the program's memory layout. Needs figuring out what &8008
is really supposed to contain (and by what mechanism) before this can be
fixed properly - left open rather than guessed at, given the last
"obvious-looking" guess (spec_852c) turned out to be completely wrong.

UPDATE - &8008 hypothesis debunked, real second bug found and fixed (but
didn't fully solve the scrambling). Fully emulated LemBoot's *real*
startup with arm2.py (tools/unpack_lemboot.py's targeted call into the
LemProg loader at &8aac, then just kept stepping past its return instead
of stopping there) all the way through to LemProg's true entry at &8000 ->
&192ac. &8008 is never touched anywhere in that trace - confirmed it's not
a LemBoot-provided pointer at all. The earlier guessed patch
(WR32(0x8008, 0x6a83c)) was accordingly removed; it never did anything
useful (matches the "no visible change" observed when it was tried).

Kept stepping the same emulation further into LemProg's own init code and
caught the *real* write to &8008: at &1930c, part of a loop that zero-
fills &14000 bytes starting from a pointer obtained via OS_ReadVduVariables
(SWI &31) for variable &95/149 (screen bank address). Our runtime already
implements SWI &31 correctly (confirmed: the real binary's first
watch_write is `pc=19308 wrote 0 -> [01b80000]`, exactly SCREEN) - the
&8008 write only ever showed up in a *throwaway test harness* whose SWI &31
stub was a no-op, leaving its output buffer at its static-image value of 0
and making the zero-loop target address 0 instead of the real screen
address. Not a bug in the real runtime at all - a red herring produced by
incomplete test-harness emulation. &8008 itself is just an incidental
address the zero-loop passes through on a *real* RISC OS where the first
screen bank happens to be low memory; irrelevant here since our SCREEN is
nowhere near it.

While chasing that, found what *is* a real runtime bug: with the &8008
patch removed, instruction budgets up to 200M still showed the CPU stuck
spinning at &2a598-&2a5a0 (tens of millions of iterations and climbing) -
a tiny loop (function F_dc48) that zeroes a data word at &2a5b0, then
busy-waits re-reading that same word until it's >=1, then clears it again
and returns. A second, similar consumer exists at &2a560 (F_2a560: wait
until the word at &2a5b0 reaches a threshold read from an object field,
then reset to 0) - i.e. &2a5b0 behaves like a free-running vsync-tick
counter that's meant to be incremented by the OS and consumed/reset by
callers waiting for "at least N more ticks". Searched the entire static
image (literal-word search and PC-relative-base arithmetic over every
STR_ site in the generated C) for anything that increments or even
references &2a5b0 by address - nothing: the only two writers are the two
consumers above, both of which only ever *reset* it to 0. The real
increment must come from RISC OS's own Vsync/timer servicing, which our
runtime does claim (OS_Claim for vector &10/Event is logged early, at
icount 69755) but the claimed handler chain (&2a70c -> F_2a5b4 et al.)
turned out to increment a *different* jiffy counter (&2a5d0), not &2a5b0 -
so nothing in the game's own translated code was ever going to set it.

Fixed by incrementing &2a5b0 once per emulated vsync tick directly in
runtime.c's irq_point()/rt_service(), the same hardcoded-special-case
style already established for &84e8/&84ec in WR32(). Confirmed this
actually fixed the hang and not just moved it: the loop's dominant
instruction-count share (pchist showing 1 billion+ hits at 50M instr/sec
emulation speed vs. 50Hz real vsync pacing) is fully explained by
busy-waiting for the next *real* vsync tick, not an unsatisfiable
condition - the per-wait iteration count (~1.7M) x number of waits over a
12s run (~608) lines up almost exactly with 50Hz x 12s.

This did *not*, however, fix the reported scrambled screen - a screenshot
taken after this fix (SDL_VIDEODRIVER=dummy, --script shot) still shows
the same comb/grid garbage pattern as scrambled.png (lost in this
session's own working directory - re-requested from the user). So the
scrambling has (at least) two separate contributing causes; the &2a5b0 fix
is real and worth keeping (it was a genuine infinite-hang bug).

UPDATE - the &8008 trail above was a dead end from a false premise, now
closed. Disassembling &192d4-&192e8 directly (the code that reads &8008)
shows: SWI &6 (OS_Byte), then F_8074 (a literal-thunk helper, R8 = its
*own* literal operand, i.e. "R8 = an address", not a further indirection)
with literal &8008 -> R8=&8008, `LDR R0,[R8]` (R0 = the word at &8008,
0x7500), then F_8074 again with literal &84f0 -> R8=&84f0, then
`STR R0,[R8]` - i.e. this code's actual job is `*(&84f0) = *(&8008)`, a
straight copy of a compile-time constant from one fixed data word to
another. &8008/&800c (0x7500/0x4200) are literal data words placed right
after the entry branch at &8000 (standard "data immediately follows the
branch, since nothing ever falls through to it" placement for absolute
RISC OS images), not a RISC-OS-supplied workspace pointer at all. There
was never anything to fix here - the value was always correct as shipped.

Went looking for the scrambled screen's real cause directly: the copy at
&e1e0-&e228 reads from fp+&124 (confirmed, by reading &19360 directly,
to be set once at startup to the real SCREEN pointer, +&2080) - and the
`0xdddddddd` watch-write we'd caught earlier lands exactly inside that
same source range (SCREEN+&21fc). So the "garbage" is being written
straight into live screen memory by a bitstream-unpack loop (&2a3d4: an
8-bit shift-extract, classic LZ/RLE decode inner loop) shortly before the
copy reads it back out - not an unrelated stale buffer. Ran a 60-real-
second (15+ billion instruction) soak test to check whether this was
just a slow progressive reveal we hadn't waited long enough for: it
isn't - every screen-memory write happens within the first ~15M
instructions (a few hundred ms equivalent) and then nothing new is ever
written again, while the CPU spends the rest of the run legitimately
vsync-pacing (confirmed: the &2a598 loop's billions of iterations scale
exactly with 50Hz x real elapsed seconds, not stuck). So whatever's being
decoded there finishes (or gives up) almost immediately and the result is
final - "just wait longer" is ruled out.

FOUND AND FIXED a second genuine bug this way, unrelated to the above:
the rendered comb/grid pattern turned out to be real decoded pixel data,
not noise - but only ever shown in two colours (dark red/black) because
our OS_Word 12 (write palette entry) handler was reading the RGB triple
from the wrong block offsets. Checked two independent real callers
directly in the disassembly - F_8178 (single-colour helper) and F_8234
(the real 16-colour palette loop, counter 0x10 down to 0, SWI &7 each
iteration) - both write red/green/blue to parameter-block offsets 2/3/4
and leave offset 1 untouched, i.e. the real format is {colour, unused,
R, G, B}. Our handler read blk[1..3] as R/G/B (one offset short),
silently absorbing the unused byte as "red", shifting real red into our
"green" slot and real green into our "blue" slot, and never reading real
blue at all. Fixed in swi() case 7 to read blk[2..4]. Confirmed via a
temporary per-call log (`palette: colour N = #RRGGBB (icount ...)`) that
colour 0 now resolves to its correct value.

That fix is real and necessary, but on its own doesn't finish the job:
after it, another 60-second soak shows *only* colour 0 (now correctly
black) ever gets set, called once per vsync from what looks like a
single-colour helper (F_8178) - the real 16-colour loop (F_8234) never
fires even once. So there's still something gating progress into full
palette setup/whatever triggers it, separate from both bugs fixed so far.
Screen is now plain black (both the real colour-0 and the
still-unprogrammed colours 1-15 happen to coincide at black) rather than
showing the old red/black pattern - visually worse-looking but more
informative, since it confirms colour 0's *value* is now right.

Chased F_8234 (really inside F_8210 - F_8234 is just a later label/entry
point within the same function, not a separate routine) further: it has
*zero* static callers anywhere in the translated code, and zero literal-
pool references to its address &8210 anywhere in LemProg.bin either -
the same "completely unreferenced" signature spec_852c had, which turned
out to be genuinely dead code. Checked the other candidate explanations
before concluding the same here:
  - F_8178 (the single-colour helper actually being called every vsync)
    always targets colour 0 *by design*, not a bug: its parameter block
    is one fixed shared buffer (literal address &8198), and blk[0]
    (colour) is never written by any caller - it just keeps its static-
    image value, which is 0. All 4 real call sites of F_8178 (found via
    direct search for "CALL(F_8178,...)") pass fixed R2/R3/R4 (one of
    them literally 0/0/0 - "flash to black"), never a variable colour.
    So this is consistently a colour-0-only effect across the board, not
    evidence of a bug in F_8178 or its callers.
  - Considered whether the missing colours arrive via a VDU 19
    (ChangePalette) byte-stream instead of OS_Word 12 - RISC OS's other
    standard palette-setting path, and one our VDU driver (vdu.c) doesn't
    implement at all (full stub, just counts unhandled bytes). Ruled
    out: a full 16-colour VDU 19 sequence needs ~96 bytes, but a 60-
    second soak (with a "Start Game" click at 5s) only ever sees 3
    unhandled VDU bytes total, nowhere near enough.

So, unresolved: F_8210/F_8234 looks for all the world like dead code
(maybe an earlier/alternate palette-loading approach superseded by the
one-colour-at-a-time F_8178 mechanism, or code for a different screen/
mode this build never reaches), and nothing else found yet accounts for
how colours 1-15 are meant to get set. Possibilities not yet checked:
whether the "Start Game" click (scripted at 5s above, confirmed to
unblock *something* - new screen-memory writes appear around icount 1.27
billion that don't happen without it) leads somewhere past our 60-second
window if given more real time or the right further input; whether
there's a third palette-setting path (another OS_Word 12 call site we
haven't found, or direct VIDC register writes gated behind a condition
we haven't met - recall spec_852c itself was VIDC-register-based and
*is* real RISC OS hardware, just seemingly unused by this build); or
whether the comb/grid pattern is in fact supposed to stay monochrome at
this stage and the still-missing piece is elsewhere entirely. Left open.

MAJOR UPDATE - the scrambled screen is fixed; it was a screen-mode/stride
bug, not a data bug. Went looking at the two non-LemProg code modules
(MemMod/TequeMemory, musmod2/MusicModule2) for clues, since neither had
been disassembled before (item 9 in the status list - both are RISC OS
*modules*, different header format, never traced). Disassembled both
directly with capstone (no spider/Ghidra adaptation needed for a quick
look - module init code is short and position-independent):
  - musmod2's init just claims its own RMA workspace and registers two
    `*Set File$Type_CC5`/`Alias$@RunType_CC5` filetype associations for
    `.CC5` ("TeqMusic") files - no vector claims, no hardware touches. A
    dead end for this investigation, consistent with it never being
    called at all (confirmed previously).
  - MemMod's init calls a "read current size, compute delta, grow" dynamic
    -area helper (OS_ReadDynamicArea/OS_ChangeDynamicArea) six times for
    areas 0-5, with target sizes {0,0,&14000,0,0,0} - i.e. area 2 gets
    grown to exactly &14000 bytes. That's the *same* &14000 constant as
    the screen zero-fill loop's size (see above) - worth noting, but ours
    runtime doesn't execute module init code at all (no RMLoad
    simulation exists), so this call never happens for us either way.

That &14000-sized dynamic area turned out to be the thread worth pulling,
via a much more direct route: read `!Run` and `!RunCold` (the disc's Obey
files - plain text, trivial to check, never read before now) directly.
Before LemBoot even starts, `!Run` does `RMensure TequeMemory ... RMLoad
MemMod`, then `echo <22> <12>` - a raw `VDU 22,12` byte sequence, i.e.
*select screen MODE 12* (RISC OS standard mode: 640x256, 4bpp/16-colour).
This directly contradicts the "320x200" screen geometry the runtime had
been using since early in this project - that was never directly
confirmed, only inferred from the game's own OS_Mouse /4 coordinate
scaling, which turns out to be the game's own internal choice of tracking
precision, not necessarily the real hardware Eig factors.

640*256*4bpp/8 = &14000 bytes/screen-bank - which cleanly explains *three*
independent &14000 sightings at once: the screen zero-fill loop in
LemProg's init, MemMod's area-2 resize target above, and (in hindsight)
why reverting the bank-stride constant from galactic dan's inherited
&14000 to 32000 earlier in this project never actually fixed anything -
&14000 was right all along, just for a different reason (real 640x256
geometry) than galactic dan's coincidental 320x256x8bpp total.

Also found a second, related inconsistency while fixing this: display.c
(display_present/display_screenshot) already hardcoded 256 rows
unconditionally, while runtime.c's bank-stride (32000 = 160 bytes/row *
200 rows) only accounted for 200 - so even before this fix, every
screenshot/frame was already silently reading 56 rows past each bank's
real data into the next bank. That alone would produce exactly the kind
of structured-but-wrong "comb" pattern seen throughout this whole
investigation.

Fixed: vduvar()/the OS_ReadModeVariable mv[] table/os_write_bank_addr()/
displayed() in runtime.c now use 640x256, LineLength 320 bytes/row,
&14000 bytes/bank, XEig=1/YEig=2, ScrRCol/ScrBRow=639/255. Also removed
the leftover galactic-dan `vdu_mode == 15 ? 640 : 320` conditionals in
rt_service() (vdu_mode is never meaningfully set for Lemmings - its VDU
driver is still a stub - so these always took the wrong branch anyway);
display is unconditionally 640 wide now.

Confirmed with raw pixel analysis, not just a screenshot: dumped the live
screen bank (recomp.screen, written by finish() on every exit) and
checked byte diversity directly - 215 distinct values across the &14000-
byte bank, dominated by background (0, ~55%) with a long real tail, not
near-uniform noise. Rendered the raw nibble data through a synthetic
16-colour "rainbow" palette (real colours 1-15 still aren't being set -
see above, that part's still open) and got an unmistakable, correctly-
proportioned picture: a row of ~8-9 repeated icon-shaped blocks (the
skill-panel icons), a row of two-digit counters under each one, and text
banners at the bottom - exactly what Lemmings' control panel should look
like. The screen-mode/stride bug that's been the subject of this entire
"scrambled screen" investigation is resolved; only the real-palette gap
(F_8210 above) remains before this renders correctly in colour.

Chased F_8210's (in)direct caller as asked. Exhaustive static search found
none - literal-word scan across the *entire* disc image (not just
LemProg), every BL call site, ARM2 jump-table idioms (`add rX,pc,#0` +
`ldr pc,[rX,rY,lsl#2]`, found and checked two real instances elsewhere -
neither is this one), and every indirect-branch form ARM2 supports
(`mov pc,rX`, `ldr pc,[...]`, scanned as raw bytes across all 147KB, not
just translated code) - none of them reference &8210, &820c (checked as
a plausible real-entry-one-instruction-earlier candidate) or &8234. It
really is dead code, same as spec_852c.

That search led somewhere better, though: re-examining spec_852c (which
*is* real, reachable hardware-interrupt code - our runtime already calls
it via call_852c(), per the VIDC section above) turned up the actual
16-colour mechanism. spec_852c branches on IOC status bits: bit 3
("flyback start") calls F_8618 -> conditionally F_8650 and F_86ac ->
F_870c (a per-component palette-fade helper - compares a "current" nibble
against a "target" nibble and steps it one unit closer, R,G,B separately),
which *builds* the live 16+16-colour source table at &83e8-&8467; bit 6
("flyback active", no bit 3) just *displays* a slice of whatever's
currently in that table to VIDC's registers. Our runtime had only ever
called the bit-6 (display) path - never bit-3 (build) - so the table
could never become anything but its compiled-in zero default, matching
exactly what was observed ("every value is zero").

Enabling the bit-3 path crashed almost immediately: spec_852c's bit-3
exit doesn't return via R14 like its bit-6 exit does - it re-reads its
own first instruction word (&852c, statically 0) as a chain-vector and
jumps there, expecting the real low-level kernel IRQ-chain installer
(&2a688-&2a6e4, writing into low workspace &100 - the same "direct
hardware-IRQ dispatch slot" this section already flagged as unemulated)
to have patched it with the next handler's address first. Unpatched,
that's a jump to address 0. Fixed with the same kind of targeted
intervention as the &84e8/&2a5b0 cases: call_addr() now treats a jump to
address 0 *while inside one of our synthetic handler calls specifically*
(the `in_handler` flag, already scoped to exactly call_852c()/
call_vector() - not a blanket rule that could mask a real gap elsewhere)
as end-of-chain/return rather than crashing. runtime.c's rt_service() now
calls both `call_852c(0x8, 0)` (build) and `call_852c(0x40, 0)` (display)
each frame. Confirmed stable over a 60-second/16-billion-instruction soak
(with a "Start Game" click) - no crash, no regression.

Still not fully there: even with the build path safely exercised every
frame, vidc.log stays all-zero. Traced one level deeper: F_86ac zeroes
the &83e8 (dest/"current") table, then F_870c fades it toward a *target*
table it reads from &8468-&84e7 - and that target table is *also* all-
zero in the static image, with no writer found anywhere in the translated
code (same literal/PC-relative search as everywhere else in this
investigation). So the fade genuinely has nothing to fade toward yet.
Given level/graphics-set-specific palettes are a standard convention for
this era, and LemGrafP/LemLevels loading is part of the ~77% of LemProg
that's never been traced (see item 5 in the status list), the most likely
next step is tracing whatever writes &8468+ - probably reachable only
from the level-loading code, not the title/panel-screen init path this
whole investigation has focused on so far.

UPDATE - found it without needing to trace the level loader: added a
watchpoint on &8468-&84e7 directly (the same watch_write() mechanism used
throughout this investigation) and it turned out to be written early and
repeatedly, with real plausible-looking packed-nibble RGB values, from pc
&198c4 - part of the title/panel init path after all, not level-loading.
So the target table *was* being populated; something else was still
blocking F_86ac from ever using it.

Found that something by tracing spec_852c's actual register values at
its three gate checks (F_8618 entry, and the two conditional-call sites
for F_8650/F_86ac): both gates read as flat zero, every single time -
despite their *static* image values being nonzero. Recomputed their real
addresses properly this time (previously assumed PC-relative ADD without
checking the U/subtract flag, same mistake as the earlier &820c red
herring) - they're not independent flag words at all, they're &84e8 and
&84ec. The exact same hardware-completion pair this project already
special-cased in WR32 to fix the original "stuck in an unemulated
hardware-completion wait" hang (see much earlier in this file) - but
that fix made *writes* to those addresses complete no-ops, so they could
never become nonzero for *any* reader, including this completely
different consumer that was never involved in the original hang. One
fix for one problem silently broke a second, unrelated piece of code
that happened to share the same two memory addresses for a different
purpose.

Fixed properly: writes to &84e8/&84ec now land for real again (removed
the no-op special case from WR32), and runtime.c clears both once per
vsync tick instead (in both rt_service() and headless irq_point(), same
spot as the &2a5b0 tick-counter fix) - bounding any busy-wait to at most
one vsync period while leaving the flag genuinely visible as nonzero to
readers like F_8618 for the rest of that period. Confirmed both
purposes now work from the same run: vidc.log shows real nonzero palette
values landing (registers 3/4/5 reaching small but genuinely non-black
RGB values, e.g. register 4 = &11), and no regression in the original
hang (60-second soaks still complete cleanly, no hang, no crash).

Not fully resolved yet: only 3 of the 16 registers have gone nonzero
within a 60-second run, and only to small (dim) values - consistent with
F_870c's one-nibble-step-per-call fade rate combined with F_86ac's gate
only firing intermittently (observed both 0 and &ff across a run, not
constantly &ff), so full convergence to the real target colours might
just need more real time than tested so far.

UPDATE - ran a 180-second/46-billion-instruction soak (with the "Start
Game" click) to check: it genuinely *stalls*, not just slow - vidc.log
still shows exactly the same 3 registers at exactly the same values
(&1, &11, &1) at the end as it did at 60 seconds. Explanation found: a
*different*, previously-unseen busy-wait now dominates almost the entire
run (pchist: &176ac, ~15 billion of the ~46 billion total instructions) -
a two-stage "wait for [&2acec]&7==0, then wait for it to become nonzero
again" handshake, reached only after the click (not present in any
pre-click run this whole investigation has done). &2acec is a new
address, not &84e8/&84ec - this is the same *category* of unemulated
hardware-completion wait as those two, not a regression of the fix above
(confirmed: no drop in vidc.log diversity between the 60s and 180s runs,
it was already stalled by 60s). Once the game gets stuck here, it stops
issuing whatever "kicks" &84e8/&84ec, which is why the palette stops
progressing - a downstream symptom of this new wait, not a new bug in
the palette mechanism itself. This is the clear next thing to chase:
same investigative pattern as &84e8/&84ec and &2a5b0 (find who reads/
writes &2acec, understand what real hardware condition it's gating on,
apply the same kind of bounded-clear fix), just not done in this
session - left open.

UPDATE - &2acec turned out not to be a bug at all, and needed no fix.
Found the writer directly: F_2a834 (literal address, already a known
function - this is the same mouse-poll routine found much earlier in the
project, used back when finding the real "Start Game" button). It wraps
a real OS_Mouse call (SWI &1C) in an IRQ-disable/enable pair and stores
the scaled X/Y/buttons into workspace at &2ace4/&2ace8/&2acec - so &2acec
is simply *the live mouse button state*, refreshed every vsync via the
already-working Event-vector chain (confirmed: F_2a834 is called from
&2a70c, the same claimed Event handler this project already relies on
elsewhere). The &176ac busy-wait (`while buttons==0: wait; while
buttons!=0: wait`) is a completely ordinary "wait for the next click" UI
idiom, not an unemulated-hardware condition - our test script simply
only ever sent *one* click, so once the title screen's click was
consumed, the game was correctly, legitimately waiting for a second one
that never came.

Confirmed by just sending a second scripted click (at 15s, after the
first one at 5s): a huge burst of new screen-memory writes immediately
follows (real level/tile-rendering code starting to run, well past the
title panel) - proving the game was never stuck, just waiting on input
our one-click test never gave it. It then hits *another* instance of the
same family of waits (0x2a564 this time, not 0x176ac) - which is simply
F_2a560, the vsync-threshold wait already identified and fixed earlier
in this file (part of the &2a5b0 tick-counter family) - i.e. ordinary
game pacing between menu transitions, not a new bug either. Palette
convergence (vidc.log) doesn't progress further within this particular
two-click test window, but that's expected: nothing suggests the
build/display mechanism's "kicks" are tied to every transition, and this
was a quick confirmation test, not a dedicated soak - worth retrying
with a longer, multi-click script if resuming this thread.

Net effect of this update: the palette and "scrambled screen" chapter of
this investigation seems to require no more generic unemulated-hardware
fixes at this point. What remains is or likely routine: more scripted
input (clicks, matching the real menu flow) to carry the game past
successive "wait for click" gates far enough to observe the real,
fully-converged in-game palette.

NEW: LemBoot is now translated and actually run (--boot), not skipped
---------------------------------------------------------------------
Up to now the runtime always skipped straight to the decompressed
LemProg, on the theory that LemBoot's job (decompress assets, chain-load)
was already fully replicated by main()'s startup code. Built out the
missing half of the static-recompilation pipeline for LemBoot itself, so
it runs for real and we can see its splash-logo sequence - partly to
answer the original scrambled-screen question directly (it didn't turn
out to be needed for that - see above), partly because LemBoot's own
logo-palette code, being much simpler than LemProg's, might show the
"real" convention more clearly than LemProg's maze of F_8210/spec_852c/
&84e8 did.

Architecture: gen_c.py gained --prefix/--tabprefix (default F_xxxx/
recomp_*) so a second translated program can coexist in the same binary
without symbol collisions - LemBoot is generated separately with
--prefix=B --tabprefix=recompB into recomp/genboot/ (see Makefile's
`regen` target, or run it directly - same pattern as run_ghidra_lemboot.sh
below). runtime.c keeps two independent {entry_tab, codemap, img_end}
triples (one per program) and a `boot_mode` flag; the active globals
(entry_tab/codemap/img_end, already used everywhere via CODECHK/
call_addr) get swapped at the exact moment LemBoot's OS_File-255 loads a
file whose leafname is "LemProg" and then jumps to &8000 to run it -
detected in call_addr(), not guessed at. Pass --boot to start from
LemBoot instead of jumping straight to LemProg; --bootimg= overrides the
image path. Default behaviour (no --boot) is completely unchanged.

Two more real obstacles found and fixed along the way, same spirit as
everything else in this file:
  - LemBoot copies its first &2000 bytes from &8000 to &40000 and
    continues running from there (see &8010-&802c) - this is how real
    hardware safely lets it OS_File-load the next asset *at* &8000,
    overwriting the low copy of itself, while still running loader logic
    from the safe high copy. Our compiled functions don't read
    instructions from MEM at all (pure address-dispatched, pre-
    translated C), so they never go stale when &8000 gets overwritten -
    we don't need the relocation trick at all. call_addr() now
    transparently remaps any jump into the relocated copy (&40000 to
    &40000+(image size)) back to the matching original &8000-based
    address and dispatches there instead.
  - &82c0 (continuation of the relocation-copy loop) turned out to be
    reached only via that relocated jump, so spider's static trace never
    found it - exact same class of gap as LemProg's &19388 early in this
    project. Seeded it as an extra entry point in run_ghidra_lemboot.sh
    (now calling export_ghidra.py directly, same as run_ghidra_lemprog.sh
    already did - it hadn't needed to before), which took LemBoot from
    29 to 57 translated functions.
  - LemBoot has its own, address-different analogue of the &84e8/&84ec
    hardware-completion pair (&87c4/&87c8, found at &8a30/&886c) - same
    "kick to &ff, wait for hardware to clear it" idiom, same fix (let
    writes land, clear once per vsync, bounded to boot_mode).

Result: --boot runs clean, no crash, no untranslated-address gap, for
150M+ headless instructions and real multi-second interactive runs - chain
-loads into LemProg correctly (detected and logged: "chain-load: LemBoot
-> LemProg"), and *does* load+decompress Krisalis's logo into real screen
memory via LemBoot's own translated code (confirmed with the same raw-
dump + synthetic-palette technique used earlier in this file - the
rendered image is unmistakably a logo, wing/ribbon artwork consistent
with "Krisalis" / chrysalis imagery).

Not there yet: the logo never becomes visible, because LemBoot's palette
for it is never set (OS_Word 12 and direct VIDC writes both silent during
the whole Krisalis-to-chain-load window - confirmed by instrumenting all
three). Found a structurally-plausible candidate - a 16-colour OS_Word 12
loop at &829c inside a small function (&8258, called from &8234) with the
exact same shape as LemProg's F_8210 - but it has *zero* callers in
LemBoot's own static analysis, same "looks dead" signature as F_8210 and
spec_852c before it, and isn't reached in any test run so far. Current
best guess: the real display/fade/wait sequence for each logo lives on a
code path spider hasn't discovered yet (most likely reached only via
another relocation-style indirect jump, the same reason &82c0 needed
seeding) - the path we currently take from &82c0 leads relatively
directly to loading LemProg, skipping whatever would normally trigger
the logo's palette and fade. Also: the chain-load to LemProg happens
*very* soon after Krisalis loads (as little as ~380K instructions in
headless terms) with no large wait in between, reinforcing that we're
skipping a step rather than genuinely waiting one out. Next step would be
the same technique used for &19388/&82c0: find what *should* branch into
the display routine (most likely another indirect/relocated jump) and
seed it explicitly.

UPDATE - found the real reason the logo was never reached (not another
unseeded entry point) and made real progress, though the logo still
doesn't fully render. The first clue was in the event log: right before
Krisalis loads, there's an unhandled `SWI &20016` (X-flagged OS_EnterOS)
with R1 pointing at &880c. Reading the real code at &87d0-&8804 (its
caller) showed why: it's LemBoot's own install of an analogue to
LemProg's &852c palette routine, written into the exact same never-
emulated low-workspace &100 direct-hardware-IRQ slot (same install
pattern, different target address - &880c instead of &852c). Confirmed
by reading &880c itself: same IOC14/IOC24-gated structure, same VIDC
write loop, same chain-vector return via an unpatched address. Nothing
was ever going to call it, same as LemProg's &852c before that was fixed
with call_852c().

Generalised call_852c() into call_hw_handler(addr, ioc14, ioc24) and
wired up LemBoot's &880c the same way during boot_mode. This surfaced
two real bugs in the existing boot_mode handling, both fixed:
  - Priority bug: call_addr()'s boot_mode redirects (relocated-copy remap,
    post-unpack vector-walk) ran *before* the in_handler chain-return
    check, so a legitimate "end this synthetic handler call" jump to
    address 0 from inside call_hw_handler got caught by the "redirect low
    addresses to LOAD" boot_mode logic instead - looping LemBoot's real
    _start forever (&880c -> 0 -> &8000 -> &880c -> ...). Fixed by
    excluding in_handler from those boot_mode checks entirely, and
    broadening the exit condition to "any address in_handler can't
    resolve", not just exactly 0 - these handler calls are injected
    outside the game's real timing (real hardware would call &880c
    asynchronously, on a genuine VSYNC IRQ, which can interrupt *anything*
    - including another asset's decompression loop mid-write), so the
    chain-vector/table data they read can't be assumed sane at the moment
    we chose to call them. Confirmed necessary: a stale chain read did
    land mid-write in a decompression target once during bring-up.
  - Added a defensive instruction-count bound inside call_hw_handler()
    itself: irq_point() (where --limit's safety net normally lives)
    early-returns while in_handler, so a genuine infinite loop inside a
    synthetic handler call would otherwise hang forever with nothing to
    stop it - confirmed this really happened before the priority-bug fix
    above (>200M instructions stuck in the &880c/0/&8000 cycle).
  - &880c's own build step (&8884) gates on &87c4/&87c8 being nonzero -
    the *same* two addresses as the generic hardware-completion busy-wait
    pair already fixed, but unlike LemProg's analogous gates (which were
    already nonzero by default in the static image), LemBoot's start at
    zero and nothing ever sets them for real. Fixed by kicking them to 1
    ourselves right before calling &880c; the existing periodic clear
    consumes it afterwards, same spirit as everywhere else in this file.

Result: runs clean under --boot, no crash, no hang, no regression to
default (non---boot) behaviour. And real progress: vidc.log shows a
genuine non-zero write for the first time ever from LemBoot's own code -
`VIDC reg 0 = 0xff` - confirming the whole chain (install detection,
call_hw_handler, the gate kick) now actually reaches and executes &880c's
VIDC-write loop with real data, not just zeroes. Not fully there yet,
though: it's a brief, two-entry flash that doesn't survive to any
screenshot (tried several, spread across a 17-second run) - the logo
never visibly appears. Most likely explanation, by direct analogy with
the *exact* unresolved LemProg mystery documented above: &880c's build
routine (&88bc, structurally identical to LemProg's F_86ac) zeroes a
"current" table and fades it toward a "target" table that's itself
unpopulated in LemBoot's static image too - the brief 0xff is plausibly
a transient/uninitialised value during the very first pass, settling
back to the zeroed default once the fade has nothing real to move
toward. Finding what's supposed to write LemBoot's target table (same
open question as LemProg's &8468, just LemBoot's own address) is the
clear next step.

UPDATE - found and fixed a genuine translator bug this way, prompted by
the user noticing that `SWI OS_EnterOS` at &87e4 really does switch
processor mode on real hardware (we'd been treating it as an inert
unhandled SWI) and asking what observable effect that has. The
instruction right after it, at &87fc, decodes (checked against the raw
word, not just capstone's rendering) to `TEQS pc, #0` with Rd=15 - the
classic pre-ASPR 26-bit-ARM "TEQP" idiom, used to restore mode+flags by
writing PC's own packed representation back into PC. gen_c.py's
dataproc() already special-cases Rd=15 for this family (TEQP/CMPP/CMNP/
TSTP, "PSR from result") but computed the Rn=PC operand with full=False
(bare address) regardless - correct for ordinary uses of PC-as-operand,
wrong for this one, where the *whole point* is reading PC's packed PSR
bits. Fixed in gen_c.py: when Rn=15 in this specific case, recompute
with full=True. This is a genuine translator correctness bug, not
LemBoot-specific - it would misfire for `TEQP/CMPP/CMNP/TSTP pc, ...`
anywhere in either program; just happened to be found here.

Separately, implemented `SWI OS_EnterOS` (&16) as a proper no-op success
(we don't model privilege modes/banked registers at all, so "entering
SVC mode" has nothing to actually do) instead of letting it fall through
to the generic unhandled-SWI path, which sets VF=1 for any X-flagged
unknown SWI - with VF left set, the TEQP return would read back as "the
mode switch failed" to anything checking it afterward, before the fix
above even gets a chance to restore real flag state.

Checked the user's original hypothesis too (musmod2 being IOC/timer-
driven rather than VSYNC-driven, installing something the main program
observes): found no OS_Claim for the Tick vector (&1C) anywhere in a
--boot run, and musmod2's own SWI chunk is never called either (0 "Mus_"
events logged, consistent with the earlier LemProg finding). Doesn't
rule out an IOC-timer mechanism entirely, but nothing currently in our
traces points at the music module specifically being the cause - closed
as a lead for now, record kept in case it resurfaces.

These two fixes together change behaviour substantially and expose a new
issue, not a regression: &880c's palette build/display mechanism (the
subject of the "LemBoot's own &880c" section above) now runs much
further, and a previously-"unreached via normal flow" loader routine at
&8b6c-&8b94 starts getting exercised - sometimes. That routine's job:
copy a filename (from wherever R1 points on entry) onto the end of a
shared `<Lem$Dir>.Lemmings.` path template (confirmed by reading it
directly - prefix lives at &8b94, filename destination at &8ba7, exactly
19 bytes in, matching the prefix's length), returning R1 pointing at the
complete path for an X-flagged (error-checking) OS_File call right after
(&8b4c, with an explicit `bvs retry` loop - the game's own code already
expects this call can fail).

Follow-up (answering "find what calls &8b6c before &8b4c fires"):
checked the disassembly directly rather than assuming a race. &8b6c has
exactly one caller, at &8b48 - a plain `BL`, immediately followed by the
`SWI &20008` at &8b4c/&8b50. &8b6c itself does the filename copy
synchronously (a simple byte-copy loop, no SWI calls, no yielding) and
returns straight back via `MOV PC,R14`. So this is NOT two independently-
timed paths racing each other - it's one straight-line call sequence,
and the destination buffer's filename segment is always fully written
before the OS_File that reads it fires. The "timing race" framing above
was wrong; withdrawing it. Also checked &880c (the fade routine) itself
for any involvement: it never touches the &8b94 path buffer or calls
into this loader chain at all, so it can't be racing it either.
Re-tested with the recoverable-X-flagged-OS_File fix in place: 5/5 clean
`--boot` runs, zero recurrences of the garbage-path crash - consistent
with it having been something else intermittent (most likely sheer
scheduling luck in exactly when a vsync got serviced relative to the
loader's own progress, not a buffer-content race), and the fix (treating
a missing file as a recoverable error under X-flag, matching what the
game's own retry loop already expects) remains the right one regardless
of the exact trigger.

Our os_file() used to treat *any* unresolved path as fatal regardless of
the X-flag, which crashed the whole emulation on this - overly
aggressive, since the game's own code is explicitly written to treat
this as recoverable. Fixed: on X-flagged OS_File calls specifically, a
missing file now returns a real RISC-OS-style error (V flag set) instead
of calling finish(), letting the game's own retry/error logic handle it
exactly as the real game would. Confirmed stable across 8+ consecutive
--boot runs post-fix (previously ~1 in 3 would crash).

Net result of this update: two real bugs fixed (one a genuine, general
translator correctness issue), and stability improved - but the logo
still doesn't render to a stable visible frame. Instrumented the actual
destination-address slot LemBoot's loader writes assets to (R9 in the
&8af0 routine, backed by memory word &8a20 - zero in the static image,
so it's filled in at runtime): it does get set to a plausible-looking
value (&01b80a00) early on and the loader completes cleanly, file loads
and all, through to chain-load into LemProg - so the loader's own
addressing isn't obviously broken either.

What *does* look wrong: watching `vidc.log` across a full `--boot` run,
&880c's palette-push step (B_8914, reached via the &87c4/&87c8 kicks in
rt_service()) does fire for real - but every push across the whole
LemBoot run writes all sixteen VIDC palette registers as zero, every
time, with no exception. So &880c's machinery (gating, kicking, build
step, push step) is now working end-to-end - it's just pushing a table
that never contains anything but zero. That's the same shape of problem
as LemProg's still-unresolved &8468 "target table" mystery, just on the
LemBoot side: B_88bc (the "build table" step, called when &87c8 is set)
is the next thing to trace - find what it's supposed to read the
Krisalis-logo colours from, and why that source is empty. This is now
the concrete next step, not a timing issue.

FIXED - the splash logos now display (Krisalis, Psygnosis, DMA Design, each
with its real fade in/out), and the "all-zero palette" above was never a
missing-data problem. Root cause: LemBoot relocates itself to &40000 and
*runs from there*, but our translated code computed every PC-derived value
against the original &8000 copy. call_addr()'s remap only fixed up
*dispatch*. Everything the program could observe about its own PC was still
wrong: PC-relative loads/stores, literal-thunk reads through R14, link
addresses. Meanwhile its absolute pointers (the &407c8 literal at &8a2c,
the &40744 target / &406c4 current palette tables at &8a14/&8a18/&86bc)
pointed at the relocated copy. So the &8a30 "start fade" store went to
&407c8 while &880c's gate read &87c8. The fade code read and wrote the low
copy, and the next asset load (at &8000) then overwrote it. "Nothing ever
sets &87c4/&87c8" was this split, not a missing writer.

Fix: gen_c.py --pcbias (used for LemBoot only, see Makefile `regen`) emits
every PC-derived value as `(addr + pc_bias)`. Function entry/dispatch stays
unbiased; Duff/SMC switches subtract the bias back. call_addr() sets
pc_bias = &38000 when it remaps a jump into the relocated copy, now also
inside call_hw_handler(), since &880c is the relocated handler too. It
resets pc_bias to 0 at chain-load. LemProg's generated code is
byte-identical (no --pcbias).

With the addressing consistent, &87c4/&87c8 behave as the code says. They
are fade-in/fade-out requests: LemBoot sets one to &ff and busy-waits at
&886c, and &88bc/&8914 step the fade once per call and clear the flag
themselves when it's done. Removed the fake kick-to-1 and the per-vsync
clears, which would cut every fade short. Headless (--limit) runs now also
call &880c on their synthetic vsync (boot_mode only). Before, only the
interactive path did, and the fake clear was what let headless runs
progress.

Second, display-side fix: LemBoot (and LemProg - see below) emits VDU 22,9,
selecting MODE 9 (320x256, 4bpp, 160 bytes/row), *after* !Run's 22,12.
Showing it as 640 wide drew each logo twice side by side at half height.
vdu_put() now tracks VDU 22 through a minimal VDU parameter-queue table,
and the display uses 320 wide in mode 9 - currently boot_mode only.

LemProg's screen mode was wrong too - now FIXED, along with its palette.
The game now starts properly: the title menu shows in full colour (with
the scrolling credits), START leads to the "Level 1 - Just dig!" briefing,
and a further click goes into a playable level. Lemmings fall from the
trapdoor and walk, and the OUT counter, timer and skill panel all work.
Two fixes:

  - Palette: &84e8/&84ec are LemProg's fade-in/fade-out requests, the
    exact analogue of LemBoot's &87c4/&87c8. &852c (F_8618 -> &8650 fade
    out / &86ac fade in) steps the fade once per call and clears the flag
    itself when it's done. The "bounded hw-completion clear" every vsync
    (see above) cut every fade off after one step, which is why only 3
    dim registers ever appeared. Removed it. Headless runs now call &852c
    on their synthetic vsync too, which is what lets the game's
    fade-waits finish without the clear.
  - Screen: LemProg emits its own `VDU 12, 22, 9`, so it runs in MODE 9
    (320x256, 4bpp, 160 bytes/row, &A000 per bank) - the "MODE 12" above
    only describes !Run. The &14000 seen in the zero-fill loop and in
    MemMod is the *total* screen memory: two mode-9 banks. LemProg reads
    only VDU variable 149, doesn't touch MEMC, and doesn't use OS_Byte
    112/113. It flips banks with OS_Word 22 (write screen base offset:
    0 / &A000, visible in recomp.events as "OS_Word 16"), which the
    runtime used to ignore. The runtime now honours OS_Word 22's display
    offset in displayed(). vduvar()/OS_ReadModeVariable report mode-9
    values (TotalScreenSize stays &14000), and the 320-wide display (VDU
    22 tracking) applies to LemProg too, not just boot_mode.

Known approximation: &852c's bit-6 path pushes a second palette (table
pointer at &83e4, versus &83e0 for the flyback push), timed by IOC timer 1
for a mid-screen split (the panel). We call both paths once per frame and
show the whole frame with whichever palette was pushed last. The title and
level screens look right so far, but a real per-scanline split isn't
emulated.

UPDATE - real-time pacing, split palette, and the interpreted &852c word.

  - Speed: the game ran at double speed. The vsync Event handler &2a70c
    calls &2a5b4, which increments &2a5b0 itself (`str r2,[pc,#-0x18]` at
    &2a5c0 - the earlier "&2a5d0" reading above was wrong), and the runtime
    *also* bumped &2a5b0 every vsync. I removed the runtime's increment.
    The runtime also dispatched its mouse event (10) through EventV
    unconditionally, and &2a70c doesn't check the event number, so each
    click counted as an extra frame. EventV now only gets events enabled
    with OS_Byte 14 (LemProg enables just event 4). Confirmed: the
    in-level TIME counter drops exactly 10 seconds over 10 real seconds.
  - The interpreted instructions: the runtime called the handler at &852c,
    but that word is LemProg's *chain pointer* - the installer at &84f4
    saves the old [&100] there and installs &8530. Once the word held a
    real address, every call (twice a frame) sent it through the
    interpreter as an instruction. hw_irq_frame() now calls whatever is
    actually installed at &100, as the kernel would (&8530 for LemProg,
    LemBoot's relocated &4080c during --boot). &2a6c4 restores [&100]
    from &852c just before OS_Exit.
  - Split palette: on vsync (IOC IRQA bit 3), &8530 pushes the top palette
    ([&83e0]) and loads IOC timer 1 from [&84f0]. When the timer fires
    (bit 6), it pushes the panel palette ([&83e4]). The latch is &7500 on
    the title screen and in-game, and &4200 on the briefing screen (the
    two constants at &8008/&800c). The display now uses the top palette
    for lines above (latch+1)/128 - 56 (2MHz timer, 64us lines, 56 flyback
    lines before line 0): line 178 in-game, line 76 on the briefing. Both
    fall inside blank bands on screen (rows 175-184 / 72-79), so the model
    is consistent. Level colours, including the briefing mini-map, are now
    correct.

FIXED - glitchy trapdoor animation at level start: an LDM emulation bug.
Every other door frame was drawn on top of leftovers. The double buffering
was fine (each bank is restored identically before drawing); the odd
animation frames were wrong. The object frame-advance code (&24eb8-&24f5c)
computes frame pointer = base + frame * frame_size from big-endian
Amiga-format fields, and the trapdoor's frame_size is &2ee = 750 bytes.
That is the Amiga size (48x25, 4 bitplanes + mask = 5*6*25), kept by the
port even though its own chunky data per frame is only 600 bytes. So odd
frames start on a halfword boundary (e.g. &6b106, where the real data is
at &6b104). The plotter reads them with `ldm r10!,{r6,r7}` (&24808).
Real ARM2 LDM ignores address bits 0-1, which lands exactly on the data;
our LDM_R used RD32's LDR-style rotate instead, so every odd frame was
read 2 bytes out. Fixed in runtime.h's LDM_BEGIN (align the start
address), in the runtime interpreter's LDM/STM, and in tools/arm2.py (the
same bug, inherited from galactic dan). Re-running tools/unpack_lemboot.py
gives byte-identical extracted_decompressed/ files, and the door now
opens smoothly frame by frame.

NEW: music and sound effects - MusicModule2 translated and running
-------------------------------------------------------------------
!Run RMLoads musmod2 ("MusicModule 1.02 (7 Apr 1990)"): a 4-channel tracker
player that also plays the game's sound effects. LemProg drives it purely
through its SWI chunk (&41C40: On, Off, Songpos, Getpos, Volume,
Samplerate, Patch, Effect, Effectstatus, Load, Effectcontrol, Run, Sector,
Bufferaddress, Memvalid, Laidback, Samplestarts). It uses no hardware
directly, only the OS Sound system. It replaces the Level 1 sound channel
handler (Sound_Configure R3 = its handler block at module+&2ea8: fill code
&2ed0, overrun fixup &2f60), and its fill calls one voice generator per DMA
channel (&2444/&2608/&27cc/&2990, via `orrs pc,r0,#2`) and runs the song
sequencer (&a78) from inside the fill. So the module is translated as-is
and the emulation is at the SoundDMA level:

  - Translation: ghidra/run_ghidra_musmod.sh + `make regen`. A relocatable
    module is position-independent, so it's analysed and translated at the
    address the runtime loads it to (MODBASE = &1800000, gen_c.py
    --base=1800000; export_ghidra.py gained a `nobase` option because a
    module's offset 0 is its header, not code). Entry points are seeded
    from the module header, the *command table, the handler block, the
    voice generators, and &2f1c (where each voice generator returns to -
    pushed by `add r0,pc,#&c`, popped by `ldm sp!,{pc}`).
  - runtime.c "RMLoads" it into an emulated RMA, runs its init, routes the
    &41C40 chunk to its SWI handler, and implements OS_Module 6 (claim;
    init claims ~150K of workspace), Sound_Configure, Sound_Stereo and
    Sound_Enable. module_call() swaps in the module's dispatch tables and
    image range for one call, so it can be entered from LemProg, LemBoot
    or interrupt context.
  - Processor modes are now modelled (MODEBITS: the mode and I/F bits of
    R15, with banked R13/R14, and R8-R12 in FIQ, in set_psr_mode()). The
    module runs in SVC mode for init and SWIs and IRQ mode for the fill,
    and switches modes itself with TEQP (e.g. &2d30: IRQ -> SVC around an
    OS_Word, whose SWI corrupts R14_svc). A SWI now loads R14_svc, and
    OS_EnterOS really enters SVC mode. The games themselves stay in USR
    mode, where MODEBITS is 0 and nothing changes.
  - That also corrects the earlier TEQP "fix" above: on ARM2, R15 as *Rn*
    reads as the bare address (PSR bits zero); only as Rm does it include
    the PSR. That's what makes `TEQP PC,#0` mean "USR mode, flags clear",
    which LemBoot (&87fc) and LemProg (&8520) use after OS_EnterOS.
    gen_c.py and the interpreter now do this; reading the full PC+PSR was
    harmless only while modes weren't modelled.
  - Sound DMA (sound_dma_advance()/sound_dma_fill()): every emulated
    centisecond tick advances a DMA clock, and each elapsed buffer period
    (samples x period) calls the Level 1 fill exactly as SoundDMA does
    (checked against the RISC OS SoundDMA source, s/Sound0: IRQ mode,
    R12 = buffer start, R10 = end, R11 = channel count/interleave, R9 =
    handler block). The interleaved 8-bit VIDC log samples are then
    converted to 16-bit stereo using the Sound_Stereo positions, at
    sum/nchan since VIDC time-multiplexes the channels. Interactive runs
    push each buffer into an SDL audio stream (no callback, SDL resamples
    from the DMA rate; a 40ms cushion is re-established if it ever runs
    dry). --wav records the same buffers.
  - Two OS details the module depends on: OS_File 5 must report the file's
    real load/exec addresses (Load checks for filetype &CC5, "TeqMusic"
    - now taken from the extracted files' ",load-exec" suffixes), and
    Sound_Configure R0=1 really does mean one channel (per the SoundDMA
    source). The Samplerate step sets 1 channel at 48us, and the sequencer
    then sets 4 channels itself on its first ticks.

Result: music plays from the level briefing screen on (20.8kHz per
channel, 4 channels, stereo positions 2 = left, 3 = right, 1/4 = centre),
along with the module-driven sound effects. Checked by recording --wav
and looking at the waveforms. The "store to compiled code" notes at
module+&1da0 onwards are its Samplestarts table, all zero words that the
analysis classed as code - harmless.

FIXED - mouse: hidden host pointer, exact hit point (touch works), and
the OS mouse bounding box.
  - LemProg draws its own software pointer, so the host pointer is now
    hidden over the window (SDL_HideCursor). The relative-mouse "capture
    for 0.5s after the game sets the pointer position" behaviour
    inherited from galactic dan is gone. The host pointer maps absolutely
    onto the screen, and when the game sets the position (OS_Word 21,3)
    the host pointer is warped there, but only while it's over the
    window.
  - The hit point was 16 rows off. The game scales OS_Mouse to
    y = (1024-Y)/4 (&2a850), but its own coordinate y=0 is screen row 16:
    the pointer save-under/draw adds &A00 (16 rows of 160 bytes) to the
    screen base (&2a9a8), and everything it hit-tests (e.g. the title's
    START slot, y 148-187) uses the same convention. display.c now maps
    host pixels with that 64 OS-unit offset (POINTER_Y_OFFSET), so the
    centre of the game's pointer - its hit point - lands exactly under
    the host pointer or a touch. Checked with a new `--script tap:px:py`
    action, which injects a host click at a screen pixel through the real
    SDL event path: the crosshair centre lands on the tapped pixel, and
    tapping the digger icon then a lemming makes it dig. Touchscreens
    work automatically, since SDL turns touches into mouse events.
  - OS_Word 21,1 (define mouse bounding box) is now honoured: OS_Mouse
    results and OS_Word 21,3 positions are clamped to it. LemProg sets one
    box per screen (&19978, &199a4, &199d0). On the level briefing screen
    it keeps the pointer to game y 80-174, i.e. below the map preview's
    palette split at line 76 - which is why the pointer used to show in
    the wrong colour when it strayed up there.
